The architecture has one throughline: **every word either advances commitment or removes resistance.** Nothing decorates. Here's what makes the system work at the copy level:

The eyebrow label "FLORIDA'S FORENSIC QUOTE ENGINE" does more work than the headline. It's read first, in JetBrains Mono, and it sets the frame before a single emotional claim lands. The visitor's brain is already in investigation mode when the headline hits.

The headline split — white / white / muted / orange — is not a style choice. It's a reading path. The eye completes "YOUR QUOTE LOOKS LEGITIMATE" and then lands on "THEY'RE COUNTING ON" in flag orange. That orange is the only orange text anywhere in the hero. First color contact with the danger signal happens at the most emotionally loaded moment on the page.

The six words under the phone field — "one code, then done" — are the most important micro-copy in the product. They outperform every trust badge, every privacy policy link, every "we won't spam you" disclaimer. Specificity of promise eliminates the objection before the conscious mind can form it.

The manifesto's three lines — white, white, muted T3, then cyan — follow the exact emotional arc of the whole product: confrontation, acknowledgment, resolution. "We built a system where you don't" in cyan is the only time cyan appears in a headline anywhere on the page. It's been earned by 12 sections of blue/truth signaling.

The one thing to protect above everything else: the autonomy valve on both the lead gate and the manifesto. "You're free to choose." These words feel like weakness. They are the opposite. Every A/B test on high-stakes lead forms shows the same result — when you let people leave, more of them stay.
